# Nomad Roasters — Tailwind CSS Landing Page

A single-file, fully responsive product landing page for a fictional specialty coffee
subscription brand, built with **HTML + Tailwind CSS** for the "Responsive Website Using
Tailwind CSS" assignment.

## What's included

- `index.html` — the entire site (markup, Tailwind config, and a small vanilla-JS script block)

No build step, no `node_modules`, no bundler. Tailwind is loaded from the **Play CDN**
(`<script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com">`), which is the fastest way to use Tailwind for a
static assignment like this — see the "Running it" section below for the production-build
alternative.

## Sections implemented

| Section | Notes |
|---|---|
| Navbar | Logo, Home/Products/About/Contact, Sign Up button, hamburger menu on mobile, dark-mode toggle |
| Hero | Heading, subtext, two CTAs, product photo, small CSS "steam" animation |
| Features | 3 cards (`grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3`), icon, title, description, hover lift + shadow |
| Products/Pricing | 3 plans, middle plan visually highlighted, hover effects on all cards/buttons |
| Testimonials (bonus) | 3 quote cards |
| About | Supports the "About" nav link with a short brand story |
| Contact | Name / Email / Message form, focus states, JS-only success message (no backend) |
| Footer | Brand name, copyright, social links, Privacy/Terms links |

## Bonus tasks covered

1. **Dark mode** — toggle button in the navbar (desktop + mobile), remembers your choice via
   `localStorage`, and respects the OS's `prefers-color-scheme` on first visit.
2. **Animations/transitions** — hover lifts on cards/buttons, a fade-up on the hero text, and a
   looping "steam" animation defined in the Tailwind config's `keyframes`/`animation`.
3. **Mobile navigation menu** — hamburger icon that slides open a stacked link list, closes again
   on link tap.
4. **Testimonials section** — three subscriber quotes.
5. **Reusable styling** — repeated utility clusters (card, button, input) are consistent across
   sections so they read as one component system even without extracting `@apply` classes.

## Tailwind concepts demonstrated

Typography (`font-display`/`font-body`, tracking, leading), color & background utilities
(including opacity modifiers like `bg-rust/10`), spacing (`p-*`, `gap-*`, `py-*`), Flexbox
(`flex`, `items-center`, `justify-between`), Grid (`grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3`), borders &
radius (`rounded-2xl`, `border`), shadows (`shadow-sm` → `shadow-2xl`), hover/focus states
(`hover:*`, `focus:*`, `group-hover:*`), and responsive prefixes (`sm: md: lg:`).

## Running it

**Fastest — just open the file:**
Double-click `index.html`, or drag it into a browser tab. Because Tailwind is loaded from the
CDN, it needs an internet connection the first time it loads (fonts + Tailwind's compiler), but
otherwise there is nothing to install.

**Recommended for editing — VS Code Live Server** (see the step-by-step guide below).

**Optional — real Tailwind build (not required for this assignment):**
If your instructor wants a proper Tailwind install (CLI/PostCSS) instead of the Play CDN:
```bash
npm install -D tailwindcss
npx tailwindcss init
# point content: ["./index.html"] in tailwind.config.js
npx tailwindcss -i ./input.css -o ./output.css --watch
```
and swap the CDN `<script>` tag for a `<link rel="stylesheet" href="output.css">`. The CDN
build in this submission is intentionally simpler for a class assignment.

